One way to create a key-value store data structure in JavaScript is to use an object.

Example:

```javascript
// Create an empty key-value store
const store = {};

// Add a key-value pair to the store
store["key1"] = "value1";

// Retrieve a value from the store using its key
const value = store["key1"];

// Update a value in the store using its key
store["key1"] = "new value";

// Delete a key-value pair from the store
delete store["key1"];
```

Alternatively, you can use the `Map` object in JavaScript to create a key-value store. The `Map` object stores key-value pairs and remembers the original insertion order of the keys.

Example:

```javascript
// Create a new empty Map
const store = new Map();

// Add a key-value pair to the Map
store.set("key1", "value1");

// Retrieve a value from the Map using its key
const value = store.get("key1");

// Update a value in the Map using its key
store.set("key1", "new value");

// Delete a key-value pair from the Map
store.delete("key1");
```

Both the object and `Map` approaches have their own advantages and disadvantages depending on your use case. It's up to you to decide which one to use based on your requirements.

