Here's an example Go program that prompts the user for an integer n and outputs the first n prime numbers:

package main

import (
    "fmt"
)

func isPrime(n int) bool {
    if n <= 1 {
        return false
    }
    for I := 2; i*i <= n; i++ {
        if n%i == 0 {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}

func main() {
    var n int

    fmt.Print("Enter an integer n: ")
    _, err := fmt.Scanf("%d", &n)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println("Invalid input!")
        return
    }

    count := 0
    num := 2
    for count < n {
        if isPrime(num) {
            fmt.Printf("%d ", num)
            count++
        }
        num++
    }
} 

The above program first defines a helper function isPrime(n int) that determines whether a given number is prime or not. This function uses a basic trial division algorithm that checks all numbers up to the square root of n to see if they are factors. 

The main function then prompts the user for an input integer n and checks for invalid input before proceeding. It then starts counting primes starting from 2 and increments the count and prints the number if it is prime. Once it has printed n primes, it stops and terminates the program.

Note that this program has an efficient algorithm for computing primes, as it uses the basic trial division algorithm which has a time complexity of O(sqrt(n)).

